Assuming you ignore all the travel woes, family stress, brutal cold, and rampant consumerism of December.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:53.820
So for our holiday special this year, I thought I'd take a break from intensive pixel measurements of The Hulk's big toe

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.440
to look at something with a bit more holiday cheer.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:01.920
The stop-motion claymation Rankin and Bass movies from the 1960s and 70s

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:05.360
Depending on who you are these are either on your television every Christmas season,

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.800
or continue to haunt your nightmares every time you go to sleep.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:10.020
Hey, Scott Cawthon!

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.220
Now that the Five Nights at Freddy's series is done I think I found you some new horror game potential here.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:21.560
These movies, which include Rudolph The Red-Nosed Reindeer, Santa Claus is Coming (Comin') to Town, A (The) Year Without a Sants Claus and Jack Frost

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:26.160
all along with their sequels comprise one of the earliest cinematic universes in known history.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.560
Literally decades before marvel made it a thing that we all talk about!

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:35.900
The producers, Arthur Rankin &amp; Jules Bass, united in their holiday movies around a series of recurring characters and themes.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:39.900
Complete with nods, sequel setups and easter eggs across all the movies.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:45.500
So what secrets could a half-century old slate of children's holiday movies possibly hold?

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.800
Oh, I don't know. How about Santa Claus' biggest secret? ((Dun dun DUNNN))

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:51.640
An untold Claus family tragedy.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.220
In the movie Santa Claus is Coming (Comin') to Town it's established that

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:59.960
Santa Claus himself was abandoned as a baby and raised as an orphan,

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.840
but what if Santa lost himself a child of his own?

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:07.880
A child who, like himself, would also have to be raised as an orphan?

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.760
It would be like Star Wars.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:10.380
It rhymed.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.320
Well, THAT is my theory today.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:18.880
That Santa Claus literally lost his young son, a poor young boy who would brave the cold of the north pole

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:21.360
and against all odds grow up to become

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.820
the intrepid explorer Yukon Cornelius. ((GASP))

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:29.300
[Singing] 'Tis the season to write theories! Falalalala lala lets go!

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:38.300
In case your family holiday traditions DON'T include spending 40 minutes struggling to get your ancient VHS player hooked up to your modern TV just so you can watch a version of Rudolph

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:45.320
your dad recorded back in 1989, COMERCIALS AND EVERYTHING, including that original Clapper commercial from way back then.

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:51.620
OR in the likely event that you were born some time this millennium and have literally no clue what these stop motion creep shows are all about,

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:56.180
Well all these 45-minute movies are available, for free, on YouTube, right now.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:01.020
Seriously! Links to all the movies are in the i cards in the upper-right hand corner of the screen,

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:07.400
so grab a blanket, some Christmas cookies, a loved one, curl up next to the fire place and watch some Christmas classics with the family.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:10.260
This theory'll still be here when you're done, just pause it.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:11.740
Or subscribe, I dunno.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:13.000
So, are you back?

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.020
Filled with the warmth of love and family and a little bit of sugar cookie?

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:20.600
Great! But for all you Scrooges who DIDN'T click, let me give you the quick, much needed refresher.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:27.280
Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer had a very shiny nose and true Christmas fashion got old-school cyber-bullied for it.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:28.560
Prompting him to run away.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.160
He winds up at Santa's shop one foggy Christmas Eve and guides the sleigh that night,

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.580
prompting all the reindeer to love him and shout with glee,

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.240
proving once and for all that timeless Holiday message:

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.380
"Your physical deformities are only societally acceptable if they serve a useful function."

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:45.060
Hey, that is a Christmas classing talking, not me!

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.880
And the rest, as they say, went down in history. ((Just like matpats theories!))

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.640
So that's the story we all know from the song,

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:52.560
but along the way in this movie we meet Yukon Corneilus,

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:58.480
the arctic prospector who helps Rudolph and the aspiring Elf-dentist Hermy in their quest of self discovery.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.660
Yukon meanwhile is on the hunt for something else,

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.180
veins of (Gold and Silver!), hoping to strike it rich.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:07.480
And also hoping not to get his tongue stuck to his pick.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:10.360
If that were to happen we'd be in a very different Christmas movie.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.120
*Muffled talking of an innocent kid* Stuck- STUCK--- STUCKK!!

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.440
But watching this movie as a kid, I could never get over how weird these moments were.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.720
And not just because of that licking sound effect.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.560
[Strange yet maybe inappropriate licking sound]

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:24.040
I mean think about it. He throws up his pick and then starts licking it.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:24.840
Why? Q^Q

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:27.120
Is he able to taste Silver and Gold?

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:31.740
His pick IS metal so wouldn't that also taste like metal?

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:36.340
Well, here's the thing, he actually isn't looking for Silver and Gold.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:39.520
In a scene that only appeared during the original airing of the film

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.740
and has only started to re-emerge in newly printed home copies,

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:46.060
Yukon has been revealed to have been looking for peppermint the whole time.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:53.020
Now all those licking sessions with his axe make a whole lot more sense.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:55.320
He can taste the peppermint.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.320
Yukon had forgotten what he was really looking for until he tasted it.

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:00.320
Why does that matter?

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:03.520
Well Yukon says he's been searching for this his whole life,

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.460
except there's one problem with that.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.040
Peppermint isn't something that exists underground.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:14.160
It's a plant that gets processed into hard candy with the one and only exception being this peppermint vein

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.360
that just so happens to be right outside Santa's workshop.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:20.460
And yet somehow Yukon got it into his head that if he digs

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:22.680
around he can just find the candy.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:25.760
It's almost like he has some childhood history living at the north pole,

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:27.920
around these underground peppermint loads.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:30.300
And then somewhere alone the line wandered off,

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.120
getting himself, and his memory lost.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:34.200
Now consider this:

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:38.300
Throughout the movie, Yukon repeatedly saves the crew from the Abominable Snow Monster

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:40.500
or, what he likes to call 'The Bumble'

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:42.960
[Didn't I ever tell you about Bumbles?]

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:44.640
[Bumbles bounce!]

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:45.300
*Convenient placed Laughter*

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:50.160
I always thought that Bumble was a strange term for this legitimately terrifying creature

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:54.440
especially when other characters expressly call it The Abominable Snow Monster.

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:59.800
But Bumble sounds exactly like what a small child struggling to say the word 'abominable' might spit out.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:02.380
And it's not like he uses it as a nickname for the monster,

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:04.720
it is actually what he calls it.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:05.640
It's even in the script!

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:07.360
'Bumble Snow Monster'

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.500
This odd vocal tick could be from him learning the word as a child but never learning the correct pronunciation

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.180
because he was lost before someone could correct him.

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:24.860
Now, as a full grown adult, he still talks like a child because he was lost as a kid and had no parents to raise him.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:27.780
But why would I go so far as to say that his long lost father is Santa?

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:31.800
Well lets start by looking at Yukon and his potential father side-by-side.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:35.940
That's a pretty decent family resemblance, but it actually goes way deeper than this.

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:39.860
Remember when I said that this theory is all about the whole Christmas Cinematic Universe?

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:44.240
Well Santa Claus is Coming [Comin'] to Town is the prequel to Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:47.420
and you should LOOK at Santa in THAT film.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:52.260
That red hair is a tell-tale sign that Yukon happens to be Santa's son

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:58.460
because in the entirety of the Rankin&amp;Bass universe there is only ONE other person with red hair,

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:01.060
and that person is Mrs. Claus.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:07.120
In all of the other scenes from Santa Claus is Comin' to Town NONE of the other villagers have red hair.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:08.900
I mean look at all those dark-haired children.

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:11.860
On top of all of that it's strange to see Yukon Cornelius

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:13.760
wandering around the north pole because, well,

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:18.220
people walking around the north pole is just not that common in this cinematic universe.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:25.820
According to the end of Santa Clause is Coming [Comin'] to Town, Santa chose his workshop to be at the north pole specifically to avoid people coming to capture him

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:27.980
because by that point in the movie he was a wanted criminal.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:29.180
Yep! You heard that one right.

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:30.620
Santa is a fugitive.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:33.180
[Wanted: Dead or Alive]

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:36.240
[The Terrible Toy Maker]

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:39.100
Ho ho ho holy moly these movies are strange. ((Agreed))

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.620
And that's even without me mentioning that acid trip scene.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:42.260
Like I said:

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:43.800
Link in the i card if you wanna watch any of these.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:46.940
Yukon being Santas son even works thematically.

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:50.880
Rudolph The Red-Nosed Reindeer is all about the characters finding themselves.

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:56.800
Yukon finding riches in Silver and Gold mines just wouldn't make sense based on the themes of that story.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:01.180
The true riches that he would find under this theory is that he would find his home again.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:06.000
And also last-last note here is that the producers Rankin&amp;Bass REALLY do love their lost orphans.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.380
It's not just Santa, but it's also The Little Drummer Boy

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.920
and also Lucas from the 1975 special

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:13.580
"The First Christmas: The Story of The First Christmas Snow"

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:15.640
A redundant title if I've ever heard one.

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:18.200
It's about an orphan who... loses his eyesight.

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:20.560
Soo... Maybe I shouldn't be making fun of that one.

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:23.620
Anyway Yukon would definitely fit in this lineage of characters

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:27.800
as the prospector who loses his parents and his memory as a child.

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:29.940
So lets just lay out all the cards on the table:

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:31.680
We have a man with a missing memory,

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:33.660
but knowledge of underground peppermint veins

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:37.320
running around the abandoned north pole where no one else is

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:41.480
talkin' like a kid and sporting neon red hair in a world where no one else does,

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:44.140
all in a story about finding your place in the world.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:46.240
But there is one last clue.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:53.740
One last hint left at the end of Santa Claus is Comin' to Town that seals the deal that Yukon is Santas long lost kid.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:56.600
During the credit roll of Santa Claus is Comin' to Town,

00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:00.060
as the mail truck drives around Santa's castle in the north pole,

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:01.140
we get this.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:05.580
A short clip of a red-headed child playing with a toy in Santa's castle.

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:09.200
I don't know about you,  but that's a small red-headed child living in the north pole,

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:13.220
who, at the time of Rudolph, is completely missing.

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:20.640
Yukon Cornelius, the son of Santa. Destined for greatness, but reduced to a side character in another hero's story.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:21.760
BUT HEY!

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:23.620
That's JUST A THEORY!

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:25.600
A CHRISTMAS THEORY!

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:28.640
AaaaaaAAANNNND jingle jingle jingle jingle jingle. [fading]

